\section{Solving}{

\itemize{
\item \code{deli_solver_not_converged}, a warning: the solver stopped without solving
the estimating equations, either because it reported a failure of its own
or because the point it returned does not solve them.
\item \code{deli_nested_solver_error}: a \code{\link[rootSolve:multiroot]{rootSolve::multiroot()}} solve was asked for
while another one was running, which it cannot be. An error rather than a
warning, because the solve cannot be attempted at all.
\item \code{deli_gmm_moments_rejected}, a warning: the Hansen J-statistic of an
over-identified GMM fit stands so far out against its reference
distribution as to all but rule the fit out, which usually means the moment
conditions cannot all hold at one value of the parameters.
\item \code{deli_gmm_moments_dependent}, a warning: the moment conditions are linearly
dependent at the estimated values, so the two-step weight matrix is a
pseudo-inverse and what comes back is the fit of the independent conditions
alone. It also carries the rarer covariance that has no inverse for want of
conditioning rather than of rank, which the message tells apart.
\item \code{deli_meat_not_invertible}: the covariance of the moment conditions cannot
be inverted, so the two-step GMM weight update has no weight matrix to
take, and \code{allow_pinv = FALSE} refused the pseudo-inverse. The counterpart
of \code{deli_bread_not_invertible} for the other half of the sandwich.
}
}

\section{The sandwich variance}{

\itemize{
\item \code{deli_bread_na}, a warning: the bread matrix holds \code{NA}, so no inverse of
it exists. A fit records no variance and carries on; \code{\link[=compute_sandwich]{compute_sandwich()}}
has nothing but a matrix to return and converts it into the error below.
\item \code{deli_bread_not_invertible}: the bread cannot be inverted and
\code{allow_pinv = FALSE} refused the pseudo-inverse, or it is rectangular and
has no inverse at any rank, or it holds \code{NA}.
\item \code{deli_summed_equations_error}: the \code{summed_equations} handed to
\code{\link[=compute_sandwich]{compute_sandwich()}} or \code{\link[=compute_bread]{compute_bread()}}, or carried as a property of
\code{\link[=MEstimator]{MEstimator()}} or \code{\link[=GMMEstimator]{GMMEstimator()}}, is not one the bread can be
differentiated from. It is neither \code{NULL} nor a function, or its return at
the point in hand is not numeric, or that return does not hold one value
per estimating equation. Every refusal of the argument carries it, as does
a \code{check_summed_equations} that is not a single \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}.
\item \code{deli_summed_equations_disagree}: the reduction is a function of the right
shape and does not sum the \code{stacked_equations} it is paired with, either by
differing from their row sums or by returning a value that is not a number
where they sum to one. The bread is differentiated from the one and the
meat is built from the other, so a sandwich assembled from two systems
would carry the shape of a covariance matrix without the meaning, and a fit
would report the estimates of the other system as well. Leads
\code{deli_summed_equations_error}.
}
}

\section{Differentiation}{

\itemize{
\item \code{deli_exact_unsupported_function}: under \code{deriv_method = "exact"}, a
function reached with a tangent-carrying argument has no rule, either
because it hands its argument to compiled code without dispatching or
because deli declines to differentiate it. \code{\link[=auto_differentiation]{auto_differentiation()}} names
the replacements to use instead.
\item \code{deli_exact_unsupported_shape}: the estimating equations arrived in a
container the summing step has no rule for, either because the tangents
survived in a shape it cannot reduce or because a per-equation list does
not hold one element per parameter, each of one length. Nothing was lost;
the shape is the problem.
\item \code{deli_exact_tangent_lost}: derivative information is gone or would be,
either because a tangent-carrying value was asked to become a plain double
or because a result arrived with no tangent and evidence that one had been
dropped on the way.
\item \code{deli_finite_difference_lost}, a warning: a finite-difference step changed
the function by less than the floating-point spacing of its values, so an
entry of the Jacobian carries none of the digits of the values it came
from.
}
}

